Hello, this is Sumanth. Today marks Day 2 of my Cloud Architect journey.

Today’s focus: Linux administration, permissions, processes, package management, services, logs, and networking fundamentals for Cloud/DevOps.
Date :13-05-2026
Day :Wednesday

1. pwd
Purpose: Show present working directory
Syntax:
pwd
Example:
pwd
Output:
/home/sumanth

2. cd
Purpose: Change directory
Syntax:
cd directory_name
Example:
cd /etc
Output:
(no output if successful)

3. id
Purpose: Show user ID and group details
Syntax:
id
Example:
id
Output:
uid=1000(sumanth) gid=1000(sumanth) groups=1000(sumanth),27(sudo)

4. groups
Purpose: Show groups current user belongs to
Syntax:
groups
Example:
groups
Output:
sumanth sudo docker

5. whoami
Purpose: Show current username
Syntax:
whoami
Example:
whoami
Output:
sumanth

6. ls -l
Purpose: Check file permissions
Syntax:
ls -l
Example:
ls -l
Output:
-rw-r--r-- 1 sumanth sumanth 120 May 13 script.sh

7. chmod
Purpose: Change file permissions
Syntax:
chmod permission filename
Example:
chmod 755 script.sh
Output:
(no output)

Permission Meaning:
755 = owner full access, others read+execute
644 = owner read+write, others read only
777 = full access to everyone (unsafe)

8. chown
Purpose: Change file ownership
Syntax:
chown user:group filename
Example:
sudo chown sumanth:sumanth script.sh
Output:
(no output)

9. ps
Purpose: Show running processes
Syntax:
ps
Example:
ps
Output:
PID TTY          TIME CMD
2345 pts/0    00:00:00 bash

10. ps aux
Purpose: Show all running processes
Syntax:
ps aux
Example:
ps aux
Output:
USER     PID %CPU %MEM COMMAND
root       1  0.0  0.1 /sbin/init

11. top
Purpose: Real-time system monitoring
Syntax:
top
Example:
top
Output:
CPU usage
Memory usage
Running processes
Load average
Uptime

Exit:
q

12. htop
Purpose: Better interactive process monitor
Syntax:
htop
Example:
htop
Output:
Colorful live process dashboard

Install:
sudo apt install htop

13. pgrep
Purpose: Find process by name
Syntax:
pgrep process_name
Example:
pgrep nginx
Output:
2345

14. kill
Purpose: Stop process
Syntax:
kill PID
Example:
kill 2345
Output:
(no output)

15. kill -9
Purpose: Force kill process
Syntax:
kill -9 PID
Example:
kill -9 2345
Output:
Process terminated

16. sleep
Purpose: Pause process
Syntax:
sleep seconds
Example:
sleep 300
Output:
(waits for 300 seconds)

17. sudo apt update
Purpose: Refresh package list
Syntax:
sudo apt update
Example:
sudo apt update
Output:
Package lists updated

18. sudo apt upgrade
Purpose: Upgrade installed packages
Syntax:
sudo apt upgrade
Example:
sudo apt upgrade
Output:
Packages upgraded

19. sudo apt install
Purpose: Install packages
Syntax:
sudo apt install package_name
Example:
sudo apt install nginx
Output:
nginx installed

20. sudo apt remove
Purpose: Remove packages
Syntax:
sudo apt remove package_name
Example:
sudo apt remove nginx
Output:
nginx removed

21. apt list --installed
Purpose: Show installed packages
Syntax:
apt list --installed
Example:
apt list --installed
Output:
bash
curl
nginx
git

22. systemctl status
Purpose: Check service status
Syntax:
sudo systemctl status service_name
Example:
sudo systemctl status nginx
Output:
nginx active (running)

23. systemctl start
Purpose: Start service
Syntax:
sudo systemctl start service_name
Example:
sudo systemctl start nginx
Output:
(no output)

24. systemctl stop
Purpose: Stop service
Syntax:
sudo systemctl stop nginx
Output:
(no output)

25. systemctl restart
Purpose: Restart service
Syntax:
sudo systemctl restart nginx
Output:
(no output)

26. systemctl enable
Purpose: Start service on boot
Syntax:
sudo systemctl enable nginx
Output:
Created symlink

27. curl localhost
Purpose: Test local web server
Syntax:
curl localhost
Example:
curl localhost
Output:
<html>Welcome to nginx</html>

28. journalctl
Purpose: View system logs
Syntax:
journalctl
Example:
journalctl
Output:
System logs

29. journalctl -u nginx
Purpose: View nginx logs
Syntax:
journalctl -u nginx
Output:
nginx service logs

30. tail
Purpose: Show end of file
Syntax:
tail filename
Example:
tail /var/log/syslog
Output:
Recent logs

31. tail -f
Purpose: Live log monitoring
Syntax:
tail -f filename
Example:
tail -f /var/log/syslog
Output:
Live updating logs

32. ip addr
Purpose: Show network interfaces and IP
Syntax:
ip addr
Example:
ip addr
Output:
inet 192.168.1.20

33. hostname -I
Purpose: Show IP address
Syntax:
hostname -I
Example:
hostname -I
Output:
192.168.1.20

34. ping
Purpose: Check connectivity
Syntax:
ping domain
Example:
ping google.com
Output:
64 bytes from google.com

35. curl
Purpose: Fetch webpage / API response
Syntax:
curl URL
Example:
curl https://google.com
Output:
HTML response

36. nslookup
Purpose: DNS lookup
Syntax:
nslookup domain
Example:
nslookup google.com
Output:
IP addresses

37. ss -tulnp
Purpose: Show open ports and listening services
Syntax:
ss -tulnp
Example:
ss -tulnp
Output:
tcp LISTEN 0 80 nginx

38. Linux Filesystem Important Directories

/       Root directory
/home   User files
/etc    Configuration files
/var    Logs + variable data
/tmp    Temporary files
/usr    User programs
/bin    Essential binaries
/opt    Optional software
/dev    Devices
/proc   Process/system info

39. Common Ports

22    SSH
80    HTTP
443   HTTPS
3306  MySQL
5432  PostgreSQL

40. Networking Concepts

IP Address = Device address
DNS = Domain name to IP translation
TCP = Reliable communication
UDP = Faster but no guarantee
HTTP = Web traffic
HTTPS = Secure web traffic

Day 2 Build Task:
linux-admin-toolkit.sh

Example:
#!/bin/bash
echo "Linux Admin Toolkit"
echo "User: $(whoami)"
echo "Hostname: $(hostname)"
echo "IP: $(hostname -I)"
echo "Disk:"
df -h
echo "Memory:"
free -m
echo "Processes:"
ps aux | head

Run:
chmod +x linux-admin-toolkit.sh
./linux-admin-toolkit.sh
